f CT = 1, DMA is currently targeting Memory1, which means Memory0 has just completed and is now inactive. In that situation, a user would expect XferCpltCallback, but HAL calls XferM1CpltCallback.

Similarly, if CT = 0, DMA is currently targeting Memory0, which means Memory1 has just completed, but HAL calls XferCpltCallback.

This makes the callback dispatch appear inverted relative to the completed buffer, which is confusing in practical DBM use cases such as refilling TX buffers on the fly.

What makes this even more confusing is that the STM32H7 HAL release notes explicitly mention this as a fix:

> Fix incorrect callback execution due to inverted CT bit check condition in HAL_DMA_IRQHandler() API.

So this looks intentional, not accidental.

