Thanks for the reply, but this does not address the issue I reported.

I am aware of the RM description of the CT bit:

CT = 0: current target memory is Memory 0
CT = 1: current target memory is Memory 1
My point is exactly that the HAL callback dispatch logic appears inconsistent with that definition.

In the driver code:

cpp
/* Current memory buffer used is Memory 1 */
if((((DMA_Stream_TypeDef *)hdma->Instance)->CR & DMA_SxCR_CT) != 0U)
{
  if(hdma->XferM1CpltCallback != NULL)
  {
    /* Transfer complete Callback for memory1 */
    hdma->XferM1CpltCallback(hdma);
  }
}
/* Current memory buffer used is Memory 0 */
else
{
  if(hdma->XferCpltCallback != NULL)
  {
    /* Transfer complete Callback for memory0 */
    hdma->XferCpltCallback(hdma);
  }
}

This logic looks reversed.

If CT == 1, then according to the RM the DMA is currently using Memory 1.
That means the buffer that has just completed is Memory 0, so the callback for Memory 0 should be called, i.e. XferCpltCallback, not XferM1CpltCallback.

Likewise, if CT == 0, then DMA is currently using Memory 0, which means Memory 1 has just completed, so XferM1CpltCallback should be called.

// This is copied from the NEW version of HAL. I am trying to prove it is wrong. 
